[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Accessing Native APIs (iOS and Android) in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the most powerful features of NativeScript is its ability to access 100% of the native iOS and Android APIs directly from JavaScript, TypeScript, or Angular. Without writing any Java, Kotlin, or Swift, you can call native classes, methods, and properties directly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Global Native Objects"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript exposes two primary global objects to interact with the underlying platform: `ios` and `android`. These objects allow you to run platform-specific conditional code seamlessly."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"utils.ios: Contains iOS-specific utilities, application instances, and delegate references."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"utils.android: Contains Android-specific utilities, application context, and activity references."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Platform-Specific Code"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute code exclusively on iOS or Android, you can use the Application or Device modules from '@nativescript/core' to check the current operating system."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import { Application, Device } from '@nativescript/core';

if (Device.os === 'iOS') {
    console.log('Running on iOS device');
} else if (Device.os === 'Android') {
    console.log('Running on Android device');
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Accessing Android Native APIs"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"On Android, NativeScript uses the global 'android' namespace or 'org.nativescript.widgets'. You can instantiate any Java or Kotlin class using the 'new' keyword and call its methods directly."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import { Application } from '@nativescript/core';

if (Application.android) {
    // Accessing the current Android activity context
    const context = Application.android.context;

    // Creating a Toast message using native Android API
    const Toast = android.widget.Toast;
    Toast.makeText(context, 'Hello from Native Android!', Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show();
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Accessing iOS Native APIs"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"On iOS, you have direct access to the Objective-C and Swift runtime through global prefixes like 'UIDevice', 'UIColor', 'NSString', etc."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { Application } from '@nativescript/core';

if (Application.ios) {
    // Accessing iOS UIDevice API
    const device = UIDevice.currentDevice;
    console.log('iOS Device Name: ' + device.name);
    console.log('iOS System Version: ' + device.systemVersion);

    // Using native UIKit to change background color of a view
    const uiColor = UIColor.redColor;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Objective-C/Swift Selectors and Memory Management"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"When dealing with iOS, remember that method signatures might differ slightly in syntax, and you often deal with pointers and memory references. Always verify API availability based on the OS version before executing native calls to prevent crashes."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript's direct access to native APIs eliminates the need for complex bridging modules for most use cases. By leveraging conditional runtime checks, you can write powerful, platform-tailored applications efficiently."}]
